        {
          "type": "doc_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.304,
            0.15,
            0.691,
            0.177
          ],
          "level": 1,
          "content": "6.891 Midterm Examination"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.233,
            0.541,
            0.255
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3/21/01"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.298,
            0.322,
            0.32
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "1 Search"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.336,
            0.818,
            0.374
          ],
          "content": "10 points Consider the following graph, in which A is the start node and F is the goal node. Assume that nodes are visited at most once."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.39,
            0.564,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.62,
            0.772,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": "1. In what order does uniform-cost search visit the nodes? 3 points A, B, E, C, F"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.812,
            0.504,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.097,
            0.817,
            0.134
          ],
          "content": "Let the heuristic function  $h(n)$  be the minimum number of arcs between node n and the goal node."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.146,
            0.72,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": "2. Is this an admissible heuristic? Why or why not? 3 points"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.177,
            0.816,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": "Yes, it is admissible because it is a conservative estimate of the distance."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.299,
            0.818,
            0.367
          ],
          "content": "3. In what order does $A^{*}$ search visit the nodes? What are their estimated values when they are visited? order - 2 points, values - 2 points  \nA(2), B(4), E(4), F(5)"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.484,
            0.529,
            0.507
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2 Clausal Normal Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.523,
            0.587,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": "8 points Convert the following sentence to CNF."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.406,
            0.559,
            0.589,
            0.578
          ],
          "content": "(A \\land B) \\lor \\neg (C \\to D)",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.315,
            0.61,
            0.679,
            0.63
          ],
          "content": "(A \\lor C) \\land (A \\lor \\neg D) \\land (B \\lor C) \\land (B \\lor \\neg D)",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.695,
            0.414,
            0.717
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3 True or False"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.733,
            0.445,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": "10 points (2 points per answer)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.763,
            0.662,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": "1. All sentences are either valid or unsatisfiable. False"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.811,
            0.505,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": "2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.097,
            0.818,
            0.133
          ],
          "content": "2. Iterative-deepening search requires space linear in the depth of the solution. True"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.146,
            0.819,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": "3. If $A \\models B$, then $A$ is true in all interpretations in which $B$ is true. False"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.177,
            0.819,
            0.214
          ],
          "content": "4. Testing the validity of a sentence in first-order logic can be done in time exponential in the size of the sentence. False"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.226,
            0.818,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": "5. DPLL runs in worst-case polynomial time in the length of the sentence.\nFalse"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.29,
            0.474,
            0.313
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4 First Order Logic"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.328,
            0.819,
            0.4
          ],
          "content": "12 points (2 points per answer) Match the formula to the English sentence it encodes (note that there are more English sentences than formulas). Let Big(x) mean “x is big” (so  $\\neg Big(x)$  means “x is small”) and let Cat(x) mean “x is a cat.”"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.415,
            0.658,
            0.434
          ],
          "content": "1. (F) $\\exists x.Big(x)\\wedge Cat(x)$ A. All cats are big."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.435,
            0.782,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": "2. (B) $\\exists x.Big(x)\\to Cat(x)$ B. There is a small thing or a cat."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.453,
            0.715,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": "3. (H)  $\\exists x. Big(x) \\lor Cat(x)$  C. All big things are cats."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.471,
            0.72,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": "4. (D) $\\forall x.Big(x)\\wedge Cat(x)$ D. Everything is a big cat."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.489,
            0.676,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "5. (C) $\\forall x.Big(x)\\to Cat(x)$ E. All cats are small."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.506,
            0.676,
            0.525
          ],
          "content": "6. (G) $\\forall x.Big(x)\\lor Cat(x)$ F. There is a big cat."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.478,
            0.525,
            0.733,
            0.542
          ],
          "content": "G. All small things are cats."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.477,
            0.543,
            0.765,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": "H. There is a big thing or a cat."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.583,
            0.561,
            0.605
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5 Resolution-Refutation 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.622,
            0.261,
            0.638
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "10 points"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.64,
            0.791,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": "Use resolution-refutation to show that the following sentence is valid."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.408,
            0.675,
            0.588,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "(P \\to Q) \\lor (Q \\to P)",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.708,
            0.601,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": "\\neg ((P \\to Q) \\lor (Q \\to P))",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.391,
            0.735,
            0.604,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "\\neg (\\neg P \\lor Q) \\land \\neg (\\neg Q \\lor P)",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.419,
            0.761,
            0.579,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": "P \\land \\neg Q \\land Q \\land \\neg P",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.812,
            0.505,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": "3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.096,
            0.319,
            0.24
          ],
          "content": "1. $P$  \n2. $\\neg Q$  \n3. $Q$  \n4. $\\neg P$  \n5. False (1,4)"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.265,
            0.384,
            0.288
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6 Unification"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.303,
            0.708,
            0.321
          ],
          "content": "10 points, 5 points per example (3 for MGU, 2 for unification)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.322,
            0.819,
            0.377
          ],
          "content": "Show the most general unifier (MGU) for each pair of sentences below. Also show the result of applying the MGU to the sentences. (Capital letters are constants, lowercase are variables.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.39,
            0.444,
            0.472
          ],
          "content": "1. $P(x,x,A)$ and $P(z,B,y)$. $\\{B / x,B / z,A / y\\}$ $P(B,B,A)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.482,
            0.532,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": "2. $P(F(x),A,G(x))$ and $P(F(z),z,w)$ $\\{A / x,A / z,G(A) / w\\}$ $P(F(A),A,G(A))$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.59,
            0.585,
            0.613
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7 First Order Clausal Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.629,
            0.818,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": "12 points, 3 points per example Convert the following sentences to clausal form."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.679,
            0.418,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": "1. $\\exists x.\\forall y.K(y,x)\\to D(y)$ $\\neg K(y,\\mathrm{Bozo})\\lor \\mathrm{D}(\\mathbf{y})$   \n2. $\\neg \\exists x.H(x)\\land C(x)$ $\\neg H(x)\\lor \\neg C(x)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.491,
            0.811,
            0.505,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": "4"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.096,
            0.364,
            0.116
          ],
          "content": "3. $\\exists x.H(x)\\wedge C(x)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.127,
            0.396,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": "$H(\\mathrm{Bozo})\\wedge \\mathrm{C}(\\mathrm{Bozo})$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.159,
            0.355,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "4.  $\\neg\\exists x.\\forall y.L(y,x)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.189,
            0.341,
            0.209
          ],
          "content": "$\\neg L(\\mathrm{foo}(\\mathrm{x}),\\mathrm{x})$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.235,
            0.564,
            0.258
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8 Resolution-Refutation 2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.273,
            0.819,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "10 points Use resolution-refutation to prove a contradiction from the following sentences. Show the two lines you are resolving and the MGU at each step."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.343,
            0.384,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": "1. $P(x)\\lor Q(F(x),x)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.374,
            0.364,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": "2. $R(y) \\lor \\neg Q(y, z)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.405,
            0.318,
            0.425
          ],
          "content": "3.  $\\neg R(F(A))$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.436,
            0.288,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": "4.  $\\neg P(A)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.466,
            0.435,
            0.487
          ],
          "content": "5. $Q(F(A), A)$ (1, 4) $\\{A / x\\}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.497,
            0.484,
            0.518
          ],
          "content": "6. $R(F(A))(2,5)\\{F(A) / y,A / z\\}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.528,
            0.348,
            0.549
          ],
          "content": "7. False (3, 6) {}"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.575,
            0.511,
            0.598
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "9 The Frame Problem"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.612,
            0.818,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": "8 points Why don't we need the equivalent of frame axioms in the STRIPS planning representation?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.703,
            0.82,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "We do not need frame axioms because STRIPS planners assume that the world is static except for the effects explicitly specified by the action descriptions."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.812,
            0.505,
            0.826
          ],
          "content": "5"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 5,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.093,
            0.578,
            0.116
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "10 Partially Ordered Plans"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.13,
            0.819,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": "10 points Here's a partially-ordered plan. Give three reasons why it is not yet correct. If any action is responsible for multiple threats, each threat counts separately."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
